\section{The combinator set is small}

The first thing to say about DSLtk's combinator library is that it is
\emph{tiny}.  Between lines 7798 and 7983 of the header there are exactly
ten public functions and operators that build parsers:

\begin{center}
\code{parser}, \code{operator*}, \code{operator&}, \code{operator|},
\code{optional}, \code{try_parse}, \code{labeled}, \code{ch},
\code{satisfy}, and \code{fail_expected}.
\end{center}

There is no \code{digit}, no \code{letter}, no \code{string}, no
\code{seq}, no \code{choice}, no \code{many}, no \code{some}, no
\code{preceded}, no \code{followed}, no \code{between}, no \code{sep_by},
and no \code{map_parser}.  There is no \code{map} combinator at all.

This is not an oversight, and it is not a gap waiting to be filled by a
future release; it is the design.  The library provides the four
\emph{shapes} of parser composition --- repetition, sequence, choice and
optionality --- and leaves the vocabulary of the language to the user.
A \code{digit} parser is three lines of code once \code{satisfy} exists,
and a parser that is written out is a parser whose error message, whose
commitment behaviour and whose whitespace handling the author controls.
Handing the user a fixed vocabulary would hand the user a fixed set of
error messages too.

The cost of the decision is that a user who expects the combinator
libraries of Haskell, or of Python's \code{parsy}, will find the
vocabulary missing.  The benefit is that the four shapes compose into
everything the others provide, and that the semantics of the four shapes
are short enough to state exactly.  This chapter states them exactly,
builds a small expression language on top of them, and shows how the
missing pieces are written.

\section{A worked example}

Listing~\ref{lst:ch22-expr} is a complete program: a recursive-descent
expression parser built entirely from \code{ch}, \code{satisfy},
\code{operator*}, \code{operator&}, \code{operator|} and
\code{try_parse}, together with a small tree representation and an
evaluator.  The language is

\begin{center}
\texttt{expr\ \ :=\ term\ (('+' | '-') term)*}\\
\texttt{term\ \ :=\ factor\ (('*' | '/') factor)*}\\
\texttt{factor\ :=\ number\ |\ '('\ expr\ ')'}
\end{center}

and the program evaluates an expression, or reports where it went wrong.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={A complete expression parser and evaluator written on top of the shipped primitives.},label={lst:ch22-expr}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"

#include <cctype>
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <string>
#include <utility>
#include <vector>

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ AST

struct Expr;
using ExprPtr = std::shared_ptr<Expr>;

struct Expr
{
  enum class Kind
  {
    Number,
    Add,
    Sub,
    Mul,
    Div
  };
  Kind kind{ Kind::Number };
  double value{ 0.0 };
  ExprPtr lhs{};
  ExprPtr rhs{};
};

static ExprPtr
make_number (double v)
{
  auto e = std::make_shared<Expr> ();
  e->kind = Expr::Kind::Number;
  e->value = v;
  return e;
}

static ExprPtr
make_binary (Expr::Kind k, ExprPtr l, ExprPtr r)
{
  auto e = std::make_shared<Expr> ();
  e->kind = k;
  e->lhs = std::move (l);
  e->rhs = std::move (r);
  return e;
}

// ------------------------------------------------- map (not in the library)

template <typename In, typename F>
static dsl::Parser<std::invoke_result_t<F, In>>
map_parser (dsl::Parser<In> p, F f)
{
  using Out = std::invoke_result_t<F, In>;
  return dsl::parser (
      [p, f] (dsl::ParsecInput &in) -> dsl::ExpectedResult<Out>
      {
        auto r = p (in);
        if (!r)
          return dsl::ExpectedResult<Out>::failure (
              r.error.pos, r.error.kind, r.error.expected);
        return f (*r);
      });
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------ leaf tokens

// lexeme (p) -- match p and then swallow the whitespace that follows.
// A failure is propagated unchanged and the cursor is left alone.
template <typename T>
static dsl::Parser<T>
lexeme (dsl::Parser<T> p)
{
  return dsl::parser (
      [p] (dsl::ParsecInput &in) -> dsl::ExpectedResult<T>
      {
        auto r = p (in);
        if (!r)
          return r;
        while (!in.eof ()
               && std::isspace (static_cast<unsigned char> (in.peek ())))
          in.consume ();
        return *r;
      });
}

static dsl::Parser<char>
digit ()
{
  return dsl::satisfy (
      [] (char c)
      { return std::isdigit (static_cast<unsigned char> (c)) != 0; },
      "digit");
}

static dsl::Parser<char>
letter ()
{
  return dsl::satisfy (
      [] (char c)
      { return std::isalpha (static_cast<unsigned char> (c)) != 0; },
      "letter");
}

static dsl::Parser<char>
alnum ()
{
  return dsl::satisfy (
      [] (char c)
      { return std::isalnum (static_cast<unsigned char> (c)) != 0; },
      "alphanumeric character");
}

// identifier := letter (alphanumeric character)*
static dsl::Parser<std::string>
identifier ()
{
  auto core = dsl::operator& (letter (), dsl::operator* (alnum ()));
  return lexeme (map_parser (
      core,
      [] (const std::pair<char, std::vector<char>> &p)
      {
        std::string s (1, p.first);
        for (char c : p.second)
          s += c;
        return s;
      }));
}

// number := digit (digit)* ('.' digit (digit)*)?
static dsl::Parser<double>
number ()
{
  auto int_part = dsl::operator& (digit (), dsl::operator* (digit ()));
  auto frac = dsl::operator& (
      dsl::ch ('.'), dsl::operator& (digit (), dsl::operator* (digit ())));
  auto whole
      = dsl::operator& (int_part, dsl::optional (dsl::try_parse (frac)));
  return lexeme (map_parser (
      whole,
      [] (const auto &p) -> double
      {
        std::string text;
        text += p.first.first;
        for (char c : p.first.second)
          text += c;
        if (p.second)
          {
            text += '.';
            text += p.second->first;
            for (char c : p.second->second.second)
              text += c;
          }
        return std::stod (text);
      }));
}

// keyword := literal, not followed by an identifier character
static dsl::Parser<std::string>
keyword (std::string kw)
{
  return dsl::parser (
      [kw] (dsl::ParsecInput &in) -> dsl::ExpectedResult<std::string>
      {
        std::size_t save = in.pos;
        for (char c : kw)
          {
            if (in.peek () != c)
              {
                in.pos = save;
                return dsl::fail_expected<std::string> (in, kw);
              }
            in.consume ();
          }
        if (!in.eof ()
            && std::isalnum (static_cast<unsigned char> (in.peek ())))
          {
            in.pos = save;
            return dsl::fail_expected<std::string> (in, kw);
          }
        return kw;
      });
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------- the grammar
//
//   expr   := term (('+' | '-') term)*
//   term   := factor (('*' | '/') factor)*
//   factor := number | '(' expr ')'
//
// The three nonterminals are file-scope objects because a recursive
// grammar cannot be written as a cycle of ordinary C++ calls: dsl::Parser
// captures its operand by value, so a reference to a nonterminal has to
// be deferred until the parser is invoked.  The `parse` member of
// dsl::Parser is public, so each nonterminal can be assembled in one
// piece and installed afterwards.

static dsl::Parser<ExprPtr> expr_p;
static dsl::Parser<ExprPtr> term_p;
static dsl::Parser<ExprPtr> factor_p;

static dsl::Parser<ExprPtr>
expr_ref ()
{
  return dsl::parser (
      [] (dsl::ParsecInput &in) -> dsl::ExpectedResult<ExprPtr>
      { return expr_p (in); });
}

static dsl::Parser<ExprPtr>
term_ref ()
{
  return dsl::parser (
      [] (dsl::ParsecInput &in) -> dsl::ExpectedResult<ExprPtr>
      { return term_p (in); });
}

static dsl::Parser<ExprPtr>
factor_ref ()
{
  return dsl::parser (
      [] (dsl::ParsecInput &in) -> dsl::ExpectedResult<ExprPtr>
      { return factor_p (in); });
}

using OpList = std::vector<std::pair<char, ExprPtr>>;

// fold (first, ops, op_a, kind_a, kind_b) -- left-associative fold over a
// list of binary operators: op_a selects kind_a, any other operator
// selects kind_b.
static ExprPtr
fold (const ExprPtr &first, const OpList &ops, char op_a, Expr::Kind kind_a,
      Expr::Kind kind_b)
{
  ExprPtr acc = first;
  for (const auto &op : ops)
    {
      auto k = (op.first == op_a) ? kind_a : kind_b;
      acc = make_binary (k, acc, op.second);
    }
  return acc;
}

static void
build_grammar ()
{
  auto num
      = map_parser (number (), [] (double v) { return make_number (v); });

  auto paren = dsl::operator& (
      dsl::operator& (lexeme (dsl::ch ('(')), expr_ref ()),
      lexeme (dsl::ch (')')));
  auto grouped = map_parser (
      paren, [] (const std::pair<std::pair<char, ExprPtr>, char> &p)
      { return p.first.second; });

  factor_p.parse = (num | grouped).parse;

  auto term_op = lexeme (dsl::ch ('*')) | lexeme (dsl::ch ('/'));
  auto term_tail = dsl::operator* (dsl::operator& (term_op, factor_ref ()));
  auto term_pair = dsl::operator& (factor_ref (), term_tail);
  term_p.parse = map_parser (
                     term_pair,
                     [] (const std::pair<ExprPtr, OpList> &p)
                     {
                       return fold (p.first, p.second, '*', Expr::Kind::Mul,
                                    Expr::Kind::Div);
                     })
                     .parse;

  auto expr_op = lexeme (dsl::ch ('+')) | lexeme (dsl::ch ('-'));
  auto expr_tail = dsl::operator* (dsl::operator& (expr_op, term_ref ()));
  auto expr_pair = dsl::operator& (term_ref (), expr_tail);
  expr_p.parse = map_parser (
                     expr_pair,
                     [] (const std::pair<ExprPtr, OpList> &p)
                     {
                       return fold (p.first, p.second, '+', Expr::Kind::Add,
                                    Expr::Kind::Sub);
                     })
                     .parse;
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------- evaluation

static double
eval (const ExprPtr &e)
{
  switch (e->kind)
    {
    case Expr::Kind::Number:
      return e->value;
    case Expr::Kind::Add:
      return eval (e->lhs) + eval (e->rhs);
    case Expr::Kind::Sub:
      return eval (e->lhs) - eval (e->rhs);
    case Expr::Kind::Mul:
      return eval (e->lhs) * eval (e->rhs);
    case Expr::Kind::Div:
      return eval (e->lhs) / eval (e->rhs);
    }
  return 0.0;
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ main

static std::string
format_expected (const std::vector<std::string> &labels)
{
  if (labels.empty ())
    return "<nothing>";
  std::string s;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < labels.size (); ++i)
    {
      if (i)
        s += (i + 1 == labels.size () ? " or " : ", ");
      s += labels[i];
    }
  return s;
}

static void
run (const std::string &src)
{
  auto outcome = dsl::run_parser (expr_p, src);
  std::cout << "  input: \"" << src << "\"\n";
  if (outcome.value)
    std::cout << "    value: " << eval (*outcome.value) << "\n";
  else
    std::cout << "    error at offset " << outcome.error.pos << ": expected "
              << format_expected (outcome.error.expected) << "\n";
}

int
main ()
{
  build_grammar ();
  run ("1 + 2 * 3");
  run ("(1 + 2) * 3");
  run ("10 / 4 - 1.5");
  run ("1 +");
  run ("2 * )");
  run ("let");

  // PEG-style ordered choice: the keyword wins when it applies, the
  // identifier wins when it does not.
  auto word = dsl::try_parse (keyword ("if")) | identifier ();
  for (const char *w : { "if", "iffy", "x" })
    {
      auto o = dsl::run_parser (word, w);
      std::cout << "  word \"" << w << "\" -> "
                << (o.value ? *o.value : std::string ("<error>")) << "\n";
    }
  return 0;
}
\end{lstlisting}

The program is worth reading for its errors as much as for its successes.
The message for \code{"1 +"} is ``expected \code{(} or \code{digit}'',
and it is wrong in an instructive way.  The parser reached the end of the
input after the plus sign and asked for a factor; a factor is either a
number or a parenthesised expression, and \code{operator|} merged the two
labels because both alternatives failed at the same offset.  The message
is technically accurate and practically useless, because the
parenthesised alternative was never plausible.  Chapter~\ref{ch:parser-diagnostics}
returns to this: the label-union rule is the price of a grammar that
does not know which alternatives were plausible.

Running the program produces:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:ch22-expr}.},label={lst:ch22-expr-out}]
  input: "1 + 2 * 3"
    value: 7
  input: "(1 + 2) * 3"
    value: 9
  input: "10 / 4 - 1.5"
    value: 1.5
  input: "1 +"
    error at offset 3: expected ( or digit
  input: "2 * )"
    error at offset 4: expected ( or digit
  input: "let"
    error at offset 0: expected ( or digit
  word "if" -> if
  word "iffy" -> iffy
  word "x" -> x
\end{lstlisting}

\section{The combinators, exactly}

Table~\ref{tab:ch22-combinators} gives every combinator with its exact
signature, its result type, and what it does to the cursor.  The cursor
column is the important one, because it is where the four shapes differ
from one another.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{The complete combinator set of the shipped parser library.}
\label{tab:ch22-combinators}
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{5pt}
\begin{tabular}{L{1.8cm} L{4.8cm} L{3.0cm} L{4.8cm}}
\toprule
\textbf{Name} & \textbf{Signature} & \textbf{Result type} &
\textbf{Cursor behaviour} \\
\midrule
\code{operator*}
  & \code{Parser<std::vector<T>> operator* (const Parser<T>& p)}
  & \codel{Parser<std::}{vector<T>>}
  & Greedy zero-or-more.  On a soft failure the cursor is restored and the
    loop stops; on a committed failure the failure is returned at once,
    without unwinding the repetitions already collected. \\
\code{operator&}
  & \code{Parser<std::pair<A,B>> operator& (const Parser<A>& a, const Parser<B>& b)}
  & \codel{Parser<std::}{pair<A,B>>}
  & If \code{a} fails, \code{a}'s error propagates unchanged.  If \code{b}
    fails, the cursor is rewound to the position before \code{a} ran, and
    the kind is upgraded to \code{Committed} when \code{a} consumed
    input. \\
\code{operator|}
  & \code{Parser<T> operator| (const Parser<T>& a, const Parser<T>& b)}
  & \code{Parser<T>}
  & Ordered choice.  \code{a} runs first; a committed failure is returned
    unchanged; otherwise the cursor is rewound and \code{b} runs.  If
    \code{b} also fails, the two errors are merged, so their label sets
    are unioned. \\
\code{optional}
  & \code{Parser<std::optional<T>> optional (const Parser<T>& p)}
  & \codel{Parser<std::}{optional<T>>}
  & A soft failure becomes a success carrying an empty
    \code{std::optional}; a committed failure propagates unchanged. \\
\code{try_parse}
  & \code{Parser<T> try_parse (const Parser<T>& p)}
  & \code{Parser<T>}
  & Snapshots the cursor, restores it on failure, and demotes the failure
    kind to \code{Soft}.  This is the explicit backtracking operator. \\
\code{labeled}
  & \code{Parser<T> labeled (const Parser<T>& p, std::string expected)}
  & \code{Parser<T>}
  & Replaces the label list of a failure with the single given label.  The
    position and the kind are untouched. \\
\code{ch}
  & \code{Parser<char> ch (char c)}
  & \code{Parser<char>}
  & Matches one specific byte.  Fails softly, without consuming, if the
    byte is not there. \\
\code{satisfy}
  & \code{Parser<char> satisfy (std::function<bool(char)> pred, std::string label)}
  & \code{Parser<char>}
  & Matches one byte accepted by the predicate.  Fails softly, without
    consuming, if the predicate rejects it or the input has ended. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Two observations about that table are worth making explicitly, because
both are easy to get wrong.

First, \code{operator|} requires that its two operands have the
\emph{same} result type.  The signature is
\code{operator| (const Parser<T>&, const Parser<T>&)}; there is no
overload that combines two different types, and no \code{std::variant} is
introduced behind the scenes.  A parser that wants to choose between a
number and a string must reconcile the two types itself, usually by
mapping both to a common representation, exactly as the \code{factor}
rule of Listing~\ref{lst:ch22-expr} does.

Second, \code{operator&} is a \emph{binary} combinator that returns a
\code{std::pair}.  Sequencing three parsers is therefore a nesting:
\code{a & b & c} parses as \code{(a & b) & c}, and the result type is
\codel{std::pair<}{std::pair<A,B>,C>}.  The nested shape is awkward but it
is not wrong, and the commit behaviour of the nested form is exactly the
commit behaviour of the flat form: a failure in the third element rewinds
the cursor past the first two.

\section{The primitives}

\code{ch} and \code{satisfy} are the only two parsers in the library that
match a byte, and they are worth reading closely, because their error
labels are what a user sees most often.

\code{ch(c)} builds a parser that matches exactly the byte \code{c}.  It
is written in terms of \code{labeled}, and the label it uses is the
character itself: a failure expects exactly that one character, so a
diagnostic for a missing parenthesis names the character and nothing
else.  The
implementation tests \code{in.peek () == c} and consumes on a match; on a
mismatch it returns \codel{fail\_expected<char>(in,}{std::string(1, c))},
which is a soft failure at the current position with a one-element label
list.

\code{satisfy(pred, label)} is the general form.  It takes a
\codel{std::function<}{bool(char)>} and a human-readable label, and fails
softly if the predicate rejects the byte --- or if the input has ended.
That last clause matters: \code{satisfy} explicitly tests
\code{!in.eof ()}, so it never matches the null byte that
\code{peek()} returns at the end of the input.  A predicate that would
otherwise accept \code{'\textbackslash 0'} is not consulted at the end of
the input.

The two primitives are enough to build every token a typical language
needs.  The three that are usually wanted are three lines each; an
identifier is a repetition and a sequence; and the one thing the library
cannot express --- changing the value type of a parser --- needs a helper
that the user writes.  Listing~\ref{lst:ch22-tokens} contains all three
of those things, and the whole listing compiles on its own.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The token parsers that the library does not ship, an identifier parser built from them, and the \code{map} helper that the library does not ship.},label={lst:ch22-tokens}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"

#include <cctype>
#include <string>
#include <utility>
#include <vector>

static dsl::Parser<char>
digit ()
{
  return dsl::satisfy (
      [] (char c)
      { return std::isdigit (static_cast<unsigned char> (c)) != 0; },
      "digit");
}

static dsl::Parser<char>
letter ()
{
  return dsl::satisfy (
      [] (char c)
      { return std::isalpha (static_cast<unsigned char> (c)) != 0; },
      "letter");
}

static dsl::Parser<char>
alnum ()
{
  return dsl::satisfy (
      [] (char c)
      { return std::isalnum (static_cast<unsigned char> (c)) != 0; },
      "alphanumeric character");
}

// The library has no map combinator, so the user writes one.
template <typename In, typename F>
static dsl::Parser<std::invoke_result_t<F, In>>
map_parser (dsl::Parser<In> p, F f)
{
  using Out = std::invoke_result_t<F, In>;
  return dsl::parser (
      [p, f] (dsl::ParsecInput &in) -> dsl::ExpectedResult<Out>
      {
        auto r = p (in);
        if (!r)
          return dsl::ExpectedResult<Out>::failure (
              r.error.pos, r.error.kind, r.error.expected);
        return f (*r);
      });
}

// identifier := letter (alphanumeric character)*
static dsl::Parser<std::string>
identifier ()
{
  auto core = dsl::operator& (letter (), dsl::operator* (alnum ()));
  return map_parser (
      core,
      [] (const std::pair<char, std::vector<char>> &p)
      {
        std::string s (1, p.first);
        for (char c : p.second)
          s += c;
        return s;
      });
}
\end{lstlisting}

The \code{map_parser} helper deserves a note.  It is twenty lines long,
it is the single most useful thing a user of this library will write, and
it is not in the library.  Its shape is forced by the result type: a
failure must be re-wrapped in the \code{ExpectedResult} of the new value
type, preserving the position, the kind and the labels, and a success is
transformed.  Note that the failure is propagated with its
\code{expected} list intact, so the labels survive a mapping --- which
matters, because \code{operator|} merges those labels.

A number parser is the same idea with an optional fraction.  The
fraction is wrapped in \code{try_parse} for a reason that the next
section explains: without it, the dot and the digits that follow it
become a commitment, and \code{1.} would be a hard error rather than the
number one followed by a stray dot.

\section{Ordered choice and commitment}

\api{try_parse} is the operator that makes PEG-style ordered choice
possible, and it is worth being precise about what it does, because the
name is misleading.  It does not \emph{try} the parser; the parser has
already run by the time \code{try_parse} sees it.  What \code{try_parse}
does is undo the parser's effects: it restores the cursor to where the
parser started, and it rewrites the failure kind from \code{Committed} to
\code{Soft}.  It is an \emph{undo}, not a \emph{try}.

The distinction matters because of the commitment rule.  A committed
failure is a promise that no alternative may be tried.  A parser that has
consumed input and then failed is, by the commit discipline of
Chapter~\ref{ch:parser-core}, entitled to make that promise --- but only
if the author of the grammar wants it.  \code{try_parse} is how the
author says \emph{not} here: this parser may consume, and may fail, but
the caller is free to look elsewhere.

The canonical use is a keyword.  Consider a language with the keyword
\code{if} and with identifiers.  The rule for a word is ``the keyword
\code{if}, or an identifier''.  If the keyword parser matches the letters
\code{i} and \code{f} and then discovers that the next character is a
letter, it has consumed two bytes and failed.  Written naively --- with
the keyword parser reporting a committed failure --- the choice would stop
there and the identifier alternative would never run, so \code{iffy}
would be a parse error.  Wrapping the keyword in \code{try_parse} says:
``if the keyword does not apply, rewind and let the identifier have a
go''.  The last three lines of Listing~\ref{lst:ch22-expr} demonstrate
exactly this, and the output shows \code{"iffy"} parsing as an identifier.

The same mechanism is used in the number parser for the fractional part.
\code{1.} is not a number in the grammar, but the failure of the
fraction is soft because the fraction was wrapped in \code{try_parse},
so the number parser accepts \code{1} and leaves the dot for whatever
comes next.  Without the wrapper, \code{operator&} would see that the
dot had been consumed, upgrade the failure to \code{Committed}, and the
whole number would fail.

Figure~\ref{fig:ch22-choice} draws the flow of \code{operator|}.  It is
the flow of every ordered choice in every parsing expression grammar ever
written, and the three branches --- succeed, commit, rewind --- are the
three outcomes the commit discipline allows.

\begin{dslgraph}
  \node[dslterm] (start) at (0,4.0) {run alternative \texttt{a}};
  \node[dsldecision] (ok) at (0,2.9) {\texttt{a} succeeded?};
  \node[dslbox] (retA) at (-4.5,1.7) {return \texttt{a}'s value};
  \node[dsldecision] (commit) at (0,1.7) {committed?};
  \node[dslbox] (retFail) at (3.6,0.5) {return the committed failure};
  \node[dslbox] (rewind) at (-2.2,0.5) {rewind the cursor};
  \node[dsldecision] (ok2) at (-2.2,-0.7) {\texttt{b} succeeded?};
  \node[dslbox] (retB) at (-5.0,-1.9) {return \texttt{b}'s value};
  \node[dslbox] (merge) at (-1.4,-1.9) {merge the two errors};
  \node[dslbox] (retM) at (1.9,-1.9) {return the merged failure};

  \draw[dslledge] (start) -- (ok);
  \draw[dslledge] (ok) -- node[pos=0.45, above left, font=\scriptsize\sffamily] {yes} (retA);
  \draw[dslledge] (ok) -- node[pos=0.85, right, font=\scriptsize\sffamily] {no} (commit);
  \draw[dslledge] (commit) -- node[pos=0.45, above right, font=\scriptsize\sffamily] {yes} (retFail);
