\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{The control flow of \code{operator|}.  A committed failure in the
first alternative ends the choice; a soft failure rewinds the cursor and
gives the second alternative a turn.  When both fail, the two errors are
merged, which is what produces messages of the form ``expected X or Y''.}
\label{fig:ch22-choice}
\end{figure}

\begin{pseudocode}[Ordered choice with commitment]
\textbf{function} choice (a, b, input)\\
\quad save := input.pos\\
\quad ra := a (input)\\
\quad if ra succeeded then\\
\qquad return ra\\
\quad if ra.error.kind = Committed then\\
\qquad return ra \quad (no alternative may be tried)\\
\quad input.pos := save\\
\quad rb := b (input)\\
\quad if rb succeeded then\\
\qquad return rb\\
\quad merge\_error (rb.error, ra.error)\\
\quad return rb
\end{pseudocode}

Note the direction of the merge in the last two lines.  The second
alternative's error is the destination and the first's is the source, so
the first alternative's labels are appended to the second's.  The set of
labels is what the user sees; the order in which they are listed is an
artefact of this choice of destination, and it is why the message in
Listing~\ref{lst:ch22-expr-out} reads ``\code{(} or \code{digit}'' rather
than ``\code{digit} or \code{(}''.

\section{Optionality and labels}

Two of the combinators deserve a closer look because they are the ones
most often used for their side effects rather than for their results.

\code{optional(p)} is the combinator that makes a rule optional, and its
semantics are a direct consequence of the commit discipline.  If \code{p}
fails softly, the result is a success carrying an empty
\code{std::optional}; if \code{p} fails with a commitment, the failure
propagates unchanged.  The distinction is exactly right for the intended
use --- an optional suffix that turns out not to be there is not an
error --- but it means that an optional parser can still fail hard, and
that failure will stop the enclosing repetition.  A grammar that wants
``this suffix is optional, whatever happens'' must wrap the suffix in
\code{try_parse} as well, which is what the fraction in the number
parser of Listing~\ref{lst:ch22-expr} does.

\code{labeled(p, text)} is the combinator that fixes a message.  When
\code{p} fails, the label list of the error is replaced by the single
label given, and nothing else about the error changes: the position stays
where \code{p} reported it, and the kind stays whatever \code{p} chose.
This is the tool for turning a low-level failure into a message a user
can act on.  A parser that matches the three letters of a keyword with
\code{operator&} reports the expected character at the point of the
mismatch --- \code{'t'}, say --- and wrapping the whole thing in
\code{labeled} with the text \code{"'let'"} produces a message that names
the construct rather than the byte.

The library itself uses \code{labeled} in exactly this way: \code{ch(c)}
is \code{labeled} applied to a raw byte comparison, with the character as
the label.  That is why a missing \code{'}'' in the expression grammar of
Listing~\ref{lst:ch22-expr} is reported as ``expected \code{(}'' and not
as ``expected a character''.

\section{Writing a recursive grammar}

A grammar that refers to itself cannot be written as a cycle of ordinary
function calls, and the reason is specific to this library: the
combinators capture their operands \emph{by value}.  Writing

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The two forward declarations that a recursive grammar would need.  The fragment compiles, but the declarations cannot be satisfied: building either parser would try to build the other, forever.},label={lst:ch22-naive}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"

#include <memory>

struct Expr;
using ExprPtr = std::shared_ptr<Expr>;

dsl::Parser<ExprPtr> factor ();   // the factor rule needs expr
dsl::Parser<ExprPtr> expr ();     // the expr rule needs factor
\end{lstlisting}

is impossible, because building the factor parser would immediately try
to build the expression parser, which would try to build the factor
parser again.

The solution used in Listing~\ref{lst:ch22-expr} is to separate the
\emph{reference} from the \emph{parser}.  Each nonterminal is a
file-scope object whose \code{parse} member is filled in later, and each
grammar rule refers to the nonterminal through a tiny forwarding parser:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={Deferring a recursive reference through the public \code{parse} member.},label={lst:ch22-defer}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"

#include <memory>

struct Expr;
using ExprPtr = std::shared_ptr<Expr>;

static dsl::Parser<ExprPtr> expr_p;   // the nonterminal
static dsl::Parser<ExprPtr> term_p;
static dsl::Parser<ExprPtr> factor_p;

static dsl::Parser<ExprPtr>
expr_ref ()
{
  return dsl::parser (
      [] (dsl::ParsecInput &in) -> dsl::ExpectedResult<ExprPtr>
      { return expr_p (in); });   // expr_p.parse is installed later
}
\end{lstlisting}

Because \code{expr_ref} looks up \code{expr_p} when it is \emph{called}
rather than when it is built, the cycle in the grammar is broken: the
three nonterminals can be assembled in any order, as long as each one's
\code{parse} member is installed before the parse begins.  The recursion
is now bounded by the nesting depth of the input, which is exactly what a
recursive-descent parser should be.

\section{Edge cases and limits}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Repetition is greedy and does not backtrack.}  \code{operator*}
  keeps the repetitions it has already collected when it stops.  A
  parser that needs to give a character back --- to match, say, a run of
  digits that ends before a dot --- must arrange for the repetition to
  stop at the right place, usually by making the element parser fail at
  the right offset.  There is no \code{many} with a maximum, and no
  \code{sep_by}.

\item \textbf{Repetition loops forever on a parser that consumes
  nothing.}  \code{operator*} calls its element in a loop and stops only
  when the element fails.  An element that succeeds without advancing the
  cursor therefore loops forever.  \code{satisfy} always consumes on
  success, so it is safe; a hand-written element parser must be checked.

\item \textbf{\code{operator&} rewinds the cursor even for a committed
  failure.}  When the second element fails, the cursor is restored to
  the position before the first element ran, whatever the kind of the
  failure.  The commitment is therefore a property of the
  \emph{error record}, not a guarantee about the cursor.  The library
  honours the record in \code{operator|} and in \code{operator*}, but a
  caller that inspects the cursor after a committed failure will find it
  rewound.

\item \textbf{\code{operator&} commits on the basis of the cursor, not
  of the error kind.}  The upgrade to \code{Committed} happens when the
  first element moved the cursor.  A first element that consumed nothing
  leaves the kind of the second element's failure alone, even if that
  second element consumed input before failing.

\item \textbf{There is no \code{map}, and no \code{map_parser}.}
  Listing~\ref{lst:ch22-tokens} writes one.  Without it, every
  combinator that changes a value type has to be written by hand, and
  the resulting code is where most of the boilerplate in a DSLtk grammar
  lives.

\item \textbf{There is no \code{sep_by}, so lists need a hand.}  A
  comma-separated list is an element parser followed by a repetition of
  ``comma and element'', and the trailing comma has to be handled by the
  user.  The repetition is \code{operator*}, and the optional trailing
  separator is \code{optional}.

\item \textbf{\code{operator|} needs matching types.}  Choosing between
  alternatives that produce different types requires the user to
  reconcile them, usually by mapping to a common type.  There is no
  \code{std::variant} in the result.

\item \textbf{There is no \code{not}, no lookahead, and no
  \code{eof}.}  A parser cannot assert that the next byte is absent, and
  the grammar of Listing~\ref{lst:ch22-expr} works around the absence of
  a negative lookahead by making the keyword parser check the character
  after the keyword itself.

\item \textbf{Left recursion is not supported.}  A rule that begins with
  itself --- \code{expr := expr '+' term} --- produces a parser that
  never terminates.  This is inherent in the design, not a defect: the
  library is a PEG-style ordered-choice library, and PEG does not support
  left recursion either.  The expression grammar of
  Listing~\ref{lst:ch22-expr} is left-associative by folding a list of
  operators, not by recursing on the left.

\item \textbf{\code{ch} and \code{satisfy} match bytes, not
  characters.}  The library is byte-oriented, so a Unicode letter is
  several bytes and \code{satisfy} with a byte predicate will reject it.
  A Unicode-aware grammar must decode the input itself.

\item \textbf{The combinator set is complete.}  There is no hidden
  combinator, no internal helper that does more, and no extension
  point.  The feature tag \code{dsl::CombinatorParser}, declared at line
  7324 of the header, is an empty marker with an empty mixin, exactly
  like \code{dsl::TaskPipeline} discussed in
  Chapter~\ref{ch:task-pipeline}; the machinery is free-standing and
  adds nothing to a derived \code{dsl::DSL} class.
\end{itemize}

\section{Closing}

DSLtk's combinator library is four shapes and two primitives.  Four
shapes: repetition, sequence, choice, optionality.  Two primitives: match
a byte, match a byte satisfying a predicate.  Everything else --- digits,
identifiers, numbers, keywords, lists, expressions --- is written by the
user in terms of those, and the whole vocabulary of a language is
therefore under the author's control, including the wording of its error
messages.

The chapter's central mechanism is commitment.  A parser may consume input
and then fail, and it must say whether the caller may look elsewhere.
\code{try_parse} is the operator that says \emph{yes, you may}; the
default is \emph{no}.  The next chapter takes the error records that these
combinators produce and turns them into messages a human can read.
}
